course: dictionary
lesson: 7793
title: Qualification and its clues
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, so the sentence around it tells you which one you need. Look for the clues. «A qualification is a certificate showing you have learned something; training at night college took one dad from £22,000 to £30,000, which is £8,000 more a year.»
explain: The sentence gives its meaning first: a qualification is a certificate showing you have learned something. The clues are certificate and learned, so the word is about proof that you have learned something, not about a game.
explain: The money shows why it matters. The night training gave the dad a qualification, and his pay went up by £8,000 a year. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check it still makes sense.
fact: A qualification is an exam pass, certificate or degree that shows you have learned something or can do a job.
fact: A qualification can also be a skill or quality that makes you suitable for something.
fact: In sport, qualification means getting through to the next stage of a competition, such as a «qualification round».
fact: «I agree, with one qualification» means you agree, but with one small condition that makes the statement less strong.
fact: GCSEs, A levels and degrees are all qualifications.
exercise: «A qualification is a certificate showing you have learned something.» What is a qualification? | A game played in the park | A bag for carrying books | *A certificate or exam pass showing you have learned something | A loud noise in class
exercise: Which of these is a qualification? | A footprint in the mud | A puddle on the playground | *A degree | A cloud in the sky
exercise: In sport, what does qualification mean? | *Getting through to the next stage of a competition | Getting a new kit | Losing your place in the team | Playing in the rain
test: «The team needed a win to gain qualification for the next round.» What does qualification mean here? | Losing the match | *Getting through to the next stage of the competition | A new team shirt
test: «I agree, with one qualification.» What does qualification mean here? | An exam paper | A prize for winning | A noisy group of friends | *A small condition that makes the statement less strong
exam: Ava has patience and kindness, which are good qualifications for teaching. What does qualifications mean here? | Lost property in the hall | Dates on a calendar | *Skills and qualities that make her suitable for teaching | A school trip
